body:
Ok, I did a little bit of a proof of concept to lerp along the actual distance of the curve, and that seems to fix the issue, but I'm not sure if there's a more optimal way to do this.

```c++

		// Recalculate the baked distances.
		real_t *bdw = baked_dist_cache.ptrw();
		bdw[0] = 0.0;
		for (int i = 0; i < pc - 1; i++) {
			bdw[i + 1] = bdw[i] + bpw[i].distance_to(bpw[i + 1]);
		}
		baked_max_ofs = bdw[pc - 1];

		// Calculate the tilt based on the actual distances.
		pidx = 0;
		real_t dist_fraction;
		real_t current_dist = 0.0;
		for (int i = 0; i < num_intervals; ++i) {
			for (int j = 0; j < midpoints[i].size(); ++j) {
				pidx++;
				current_dist = bdw[pidx];
				dist_fraction = current_dist / baked_max_ofs; // TODO! Proof of concept -- need to calculate the segment distance, not the whole curve dist.  This will only work with curves that have 2 points.
				if (!closed || i < num_intervals - 1) {
					btw[pidx] = Math::lerp(points[i].tilt, points[i + 1].tilt, dist_fraction);
				} else {
					btw[pidx] = Math::lerp(points[i].tilt, points[0].tilt, dist_fraction);
				}
			}
		}
```

Unfortunately, to implement it completely, I'd have to iterate over all the midpoints and calculate the length of each segment.  It'd probably be a lot more optimal if we can just figure out the correct lerp weight value in the original code instead of using E.key.

This appears to fix the issue for a curve with just 2 points, though, so it at least demonstrates where the bug is.
